{"name":"Rusty Coin","hex":"#8d5f2c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rusty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Marked or corroded by rust. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Of the rust color, reddish or reddish-brown."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A gun or in particular an old or worn one."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rusty"},{"term":"coin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of currency, usually metallic and in the shape of a disc, but sometimes polygonal, or with a hole in the middle.","A token used in a special establishment like a casino."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make of a definite fineness, and convert into coins, as a mass of metal.","To make or fabricate (especially a word or phrase)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coin","blocks":[{"text":"A coin is a small object, usually round and flat, used primarily as a medium of exchange or legal tender. They are standardized in weight, and produced in large quantities at a mint in order to facilitate trade. They are most often issued by a government. Coins often have images, numerals, or text on them. The faces of coins or medals are sometimes called the obverse and the reverse, referring to the front and back sides, respectively. The obverse of a coin is commonly called heads, because it often depicts the head of a prominent person, and the reverse is known as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Kiloware.JPG/330px-Kiloware.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coin"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/KINGS_of_LYDIA._Alyattes._Circa_620-10-564-53_BC.jpg/500px-KINGS_of_LYDIA._Alyattes._Circa_620-10-564-53_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/KINGS_of_LYDIA._Alyattes._Circa_620-10-564-53_BC.jpg/500px-KINGS_of_LYDIA._Alyattes._Circa_620-10-564-53_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coin of Alyattes of Lydia, c. 620/10–564/53 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKINGS_of_LYDIA._Alyattes._Circa_620-10-564-53_BC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Triti%2C_Phanes%2C_625-600_BC%2C_Ionia_-_301224.jpg/500px-Triti%2C_Phanes%2C_625-600_BC%2C_Ionia_-_301224.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Triti%2C_Phanes%2C_625-600_BC%2C_Ionia_-_301224.jpg/500px-Triti%2C_Phanes%2C_625-600_BC%2C_Ionia_-_301224.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The earliest inscribed coinage: electrum coin of Phanes from Ephesus, 625–600 BC. Obverse: Stag grazing right, ΦΑΝΕΩΣ (retrograde). Reverse: Two incuse punches, each with raised intersecting lines.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATriti%2C_Phanes%2C_625-600_BC%2C_Ionia_-_301224.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg/500px-Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg/500px-Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An oxhide ingot from Crete. Late Bronze Age metal ingots were given standard shapes, such as the shape of an \"ox-hide\", suggesting that they represented standardized values.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMinoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/KING